The EE scheduler hands the IOP its cycles in batches of one emulator slice

What it fixes: the scheduler fed the IOP emulator `eeCycles / 8` at every generated-code checkpoint
(EeScheduler::accountCycles), and the generated code checkpoints every 8 EE cycles
(EeScheduler::kGuestDispatchCycles = 8). IopEmulator::runEeCycles therefore called Impl::runCycles with a
budget of ONE cycle, and every such call pays fixed work: syncHostSpu2 (and the host SPU2's advance),
servicePendingDmaInterrupts, servicePendingGuestCallbacks, timrman.serviceDue and IopKernel::beginNextReady
(a scan of the IOP thread map), plus at most one interpreted instruction. That fixed work was paid tens of
millions of times per emulated second whenever any IOP thread was ready, and it dominated the frame: at the
game's memory-card dialog - where the game polls the card about once per frame through its own recompiled
SDK RPC - a 60 s run produced only 5 to 7 frames and 7 to 9 vertical blanks per second instead of the
console's 60, and every end-of-run backtrace had the game thread in IopKernel::beginNextReady (measured in
T-108).

What it does:
  * `IopSubsystem::sliceEeCycles()` (new, public) says how many EE cycles one full emulator slice is worth:
    the emulator's own kDefaultSlice (256 IOP instructions) times the console's clock ratio, now a named
    constant `kEeCyclesPerIopCycle = 8` that runEeCycles uses as well. One slice is 2048 EE cycles.
  * The scheduler keeps the IOP's pending cycles as a debt in EE cycles (m_iopPendingEeCycles) and hands
    them over only when the debt reaches one slice (`EeScheduler::flushIopDebt`). The totals are exactly
    what they were: the debt is handed over in full and `IopSubsystem::runEeCycles` still divides by 8 and
    keeps the remainder, so a given number of EE cycles gives exactly the same number of IOP cycles as
    before - nothing lost, nothing added (checked with the host SPU2's cycle counter, identical before and
    after).
  * `EeScheduler::iopBatchStats()` (new, EeIopBatchStats) exposes batchEeCycles, pendingEeCycles, runs and
    eeCycles: how often the IOP was run, with how many EE cycles in total, and what is still owed.
  * A diagnostic, `DON_IOP_BATCH_EE_CYCLES=<n>` (read once in the scheduler's constructor), forces the
    batch size in EE cycles. The default is one slice; `8` restores the pre-0033 feed exactly (one run per
    checkpoint, measured: 640081 runs for 640000 checkpoints, the same number the unpatched tree gives),
    which is how the before/after frame and CPU figures of T-108 were taken with one binary.

Flush points - the debt is handed over in full at the moments the EE side may depend on the IOP's state
being current:
  1. before a vertical-blank event is processed (EeScheduler::processEvent, the VBlankStart case);
  2. before the scheduler goes idle (EeScheduler::waitForEvent, at its top) and again after the idle time
     has been accounted there, so the IOP runs for the idle EE cycles exactly as it did when it was fed
     every checkpoint;
  3. before the EE side reaches the IOP subsystem synchronously: PS2Runtime::loadIopModule,
     loadIopModuleBuffer, stopIopModule, selectIopRpcAbi, canBindIopRpc, handleIopRpc, notifyIopSifTransfer,
     allocateIopMemory, freeIopMemory, readIopMemory, writeIopMemory, zeroIopMemory and isIopMemoryRange.
     One helper, PS2Runtime::flushIopDebtBeforeEeIopAccess, is called at the top of each of them; it does
     nothing while the IOP is running (m_inIopCycles, set around the runEeCycles call), because the IOP's
     own host callbacks (those same memory accessors) come back through this object and must not re-enter
     the emulator.
  Not flushed: resetIop, iopDebugSnapshot (the debug panel is not the guest) and, of course, runEeCycles
  itself. Nothing else changes: the 8-cycle checkpoint of the generated code, the emulator's own slice
  logic and the order of events inside a slice are untouched.

Measured (tests):
  * tests/runtime/iop_batch_proxy_test.cpp (new) measures the IOP from OUTSIDE, through the host SPU2 hook
    (one advance per emulator slice), with a guest that runs 640000 checkpoints (5.12M EE cycles) and
    crosses a vertical blank: before this patch `iop advances=640081` for 640000 checkpoints (one run per
    checkpoint); with it `advances=2579` for 9672 slices (one run per slice plus the flush points crossed).
    The cycles the host SPU2 sees are identical in both (2476081 = EE cycles / 8), so the ratio is
    preserved exactly.
  * tests/runtime/iop_batch_test.cpp (new) runs the same guest and reads EeScheduler::iopBatchStats:
    batchEeCycles 2048, 2508 runs for 19808652 EE cycles handed over, handed over + pending = the accounted
    EE cycles, pending 0 when the run ends, and at most one checkpoint owed right after the vertical blank
    (the flush point works).
  * The rest of tests/runtime still passes (11 tests), in particular iop_host_spu2_test.cpp, which checks
    the cycle totals a host SPU2 receives.

Measured (game, at the memory-card dialog, software renderer):
  * three 60 s runs: RESULT: OK_VSYNC_LOOP 840 960 each (840 frames, 960 vertical blanks); before this
    patch the same runs gave 300-420 frames and 420-540 vertical blanks. The console's 60 per second is
    still not reached (16 vertical blanks per second), and where the time goes now - 8 end-of-run
    backtraces: 5 in the interpreted IOP (IopCpuCore::executeInstruction and the IOP's DMA/interrupt
    service), 2 in the EE scheduler's per-dispatch bookkeeping (EeScheduler::processDueDeadlines reading
    the clock, EeScheduler::processPendingEvents taking its event mutex), 1 in the VU1 interpreter - is a
    different topic and is not touched here.
  * the picture is byte-identical to the one taken before this patch (image_parity: SSIM=1.0000, MAD=0.00),
    the RAM dump comparison still shows the same three differing words in 0x4D69B0-0x4D6A10, and the boot
    is the same (14 module loads, the binds, the dialog).
  * CPU of the process during a 60 s run: after 58.06 s user + 0.29 s system, 96% of a core (60.26 s wall);
    before (DON_IOP_BATCH_EE_CYCLES=8) 59.77 s user + 0.24 s system, 99% (60.15 s wall). The port is
    compute-bound in both cases: the patch does more frames per second with the same core, it does not wait.

How it was verified: both new tests fail against the tree without this patch - the proxy one on two checks
(the IOP run 640081 times for 640000 checkpoints), the counter one because its accessor does not exist
there - and pass with it; the rest of tests/runtime still passes. The separate fix of
tools/run/classify_log.py's progress pattern (a `[don-ram]`/`[don-gs]` diagnostic is not progress) is not
part of this patch.
